why the answer should be invalid. The argument\'s conclusion is the converse of the premise, but converse isn\'t equivalent to the original implication. Therefore, the argument is not deductively valid. \n\nAlternatively, another way to see it is that the argument is attempting to derive the converse of a conditional statement from the original conditional. Since you can\'t validly derive the converse from the original implication alone. You need additional premises for that. Like, to go from "if P then Q" to "if not Q then not P," you need additional info, perhaps that the relation is bidirectional or something. But here, there\'s no such info given. So yeah, the argument is invalid.\n</think>\n\nThe argument is **invalid**.\n\n### Step-by-Step Explanation:\n1. **Premise Analysis**:\n   - The first premise states: *"If someone is a schoolmate of Sondra (\\(S(x)\\)), then they are not a stepsister of Pricilla (\\(¬\\text{St}(x)\\))":  \n     \\(S(x) \\rightarrow ¬\\text{St}(x)\\).  \n   - This is logically equivalent to: \\(¬S(x) \\lor ¬\\text{St}(x)\\).\n\n2. **Conclusion Analysis**:\n   - The conclusion reverses the implication: *"If someone is not a stepsister of Pricilla (\\(¬\\text{St}(x)\\)), then they are a schoolmate of Sondra (\\(S(x)\\))":  \n     \\(¬\\text{St}(x) \\rightarrow S(x)\\), which simplifies to \\(St(x) \\lor S(x)\\).\n\n3. **Logical Equivalence Check**:\n   - The premise \\(S(x) \\rightarrow ¬\\text{St}(x)\\) (\\(¬S(x) \\lor ¬\\text{St}(x)\\)) is **not** equivalent to the conclusion \\(¬\\text{St}(x) \\rightarrow S(x)\\) (\\(St(x) \\lor S(x)\\)).  \n   - While \\(¬S(x) \\lor ¬\\text{St}(x)\\) and \\(S(x) \\lor \\text{St}(x)\\) are both **contradictions** of each other in specific cases (see truth table below), they are not logically equivalent.\n\n4. **Truth Table Verification**:\n   | \\(S(x)\\) | \\(\\text{St}(x)\\) | \\(S(x) \\rightarrow ¬\\text{St}(x)\\) | \\(¬\\text{St}(x) \\rightarrow S(x)\\) |\n   |----------|------------------|--------